  palette:
    bg: 0
    wire: 8 (main segments), 5 (crossing markers, see crossings below), 9 (rectangular filled blocks embedded in wire)
    token_colors: 4-5 distinct colors from {1,2,3,4,6,7}, one per channel
  layout: N=4-5 channels; tokens on opposite sides of grid (top/bottom [observed 3/4] or left/right); uniform column spacing 4-5 between channel centers
  tokens:
    shapes: solid 3x3 block, X-pattern (diags+center, 5 cells), diamond (4 cardinal no center, 4 cells)
    input: each color appears at most once as solid or X; 0-2 X tokens, rest solid; X channel far-end is usually empty (~67%, diamond generated by solver routing) or has pre-placed diamond (~33%)
    output: every channel has diamond on one side and X on the other; same color pairs linked by wire routing
  wire_network:
    composition: 1 connected component (rarely 2 for vertical layout [observed]); 50-160 cells total
    structure: orthogonal segments of color 8; color-9 rectangular blocks (0-4 blocks, 2x2 to 5x4) embedded in wire as wide junctions; each rect has 1-3 wires passing through (50% 1-wire, 38% 2-wire, 12% 3-wire); wires pass straight through (entry face → opposite face); exception: when rect has exactly 1 entry + 1 exit on adjacent (not opposite) faces, wire turns inside
    crossings (color 5): each perpendicular wire intersection is bracketed by a pair of 5-cells on the through-wire, one cell before and after the crossing point, marking "continue straight, do not turn"
    endpoints: each channel contributes 1 endpoint per side; endpoint is degree-1 cell; token center is 2 cells outward from endpoint in cardinal exit direction
    routing: wire implements a permutation of channels; at least some channels must cross (non-identity permutation) [observed]
  transform: |
    solid: becomes diamond in-place + X emitted to far endpoint via wire
    X: unchanged + diamond emitted to far endpoint via wire
    diamond: unchanged (already at rest)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid H,W in [20,26]. Pick N in {4,5} channel columns with uniform spacing 4-5, centered in grid. Token rows at row 2 (top) and row H-4 to H-3 (bottom).
    2. Assign N distinct token colors. Choose a random permutation P of channels (non-identity). For each channel i, diamond of color i at top, X of color P(i) at bottom (output state).
    3. Build wire network connecting top endpoints to bottom endpoints implementing permutation P. Use manhattan routing with vertical column segments and horizontal crossover segments. At each perpendicular wire crossing, place a pair of color-5 cells on the through-wire flanking the intersection. Embed 0-3 color-9 rectangular blocks (2x2 to 5x4) along wire segments: pick a contiguous rectangle of existing wire cells (color 8) that covers 1-3 parallel wires, replace those cells with color 9. Each wire entering the rect must exit from the opposite face (straight pass-through). Exception: if the rect has exactly 1 entry + 1 exit on adjacent faces, the wire turns inside.
    4. Derive input from output -- for each channel, randomly choose which side gets the input token. Most tokens are solid (reverses diamond to solid at that position). Pick 0-2 channels to have X input token instead; for X channels, far-end is usually left empty (~67%) or has pre-placed diamond (~33%). Remaining non-X far-end positions are left empty (bg).
    5. Oracle -- run solver, verify output matches expected.
